From 58bb3964270e32bced487b186fd9eda3f3095c6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 05:17:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(rust-rounds): queue a week kept before home could read it
 (RR25)

A `/2` week that reached home while a build that read only `/1` ran was
kept as any `brief` file is, with nothing queued. The mailbox never lists
it again (one name holds one body, and the cursor is past it), so after
the upgrade GET /rust-weeks/:id served it with `runs: []` for good.

- The first pass of each process queues the programs of every week of
  Rust Rounds home holds that reads as its name says
  (`mailbox::pass::queue_held_weeks`), under the deletion hold in one
  `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction, as `keep` queues a new one. An upgrade
  is a restart, so the first pass after it queues what the older build
  left. `LastIndexes::weeks_queued` remembers it for the process; a
  failed sweep is tried again next pass.
- Queueing is idempotent (`ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` on the program's
  hash), so a week queued already writes nothing. `queue_week` now
  returns the rows it wrote, and the sweep logs that count only when it
  is not zero.
- `keep` is unchanged: a re-put of the same bytes never reaches it, since
  the read loop skips a file home holds with the same SHA-256.
- The docs that said home never holds a week whose programs go
  unrecorded (`keep`, `queue_week`, docs/identity.md) now say how it
  holds: from the transaction that keeps a week home reads, or from the
  first pass after the upgrade for one kept before.

